<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aurich</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aurich is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upstalsboom">Upstalsboom</a> was a meeting place of the medieval Frisian communities, and from the 16th century Aurich was the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a> counts and princes of East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>. It remained the administrative centre of the region under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurich.jpg" alt="Aurich" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olaf2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fussg%C3%A4ngerzone_Aurich.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
